# agent-host-boot.sh.schema
#
# Companion document for `agent-host-boot.sh`, the program the deployment names
# as the container's entrypoint.
#
# ## Environment contract
#
# | Variable | Required | Default | Meaning |
# |----------|----------|---------|---------|
# | `AGENT_IDS` | yes | — | The agents this host runs, comma-separated. Each id becomes one herdr workspace (label = id), one workspace directory, one home, one roster line. |
# | `AGENT_TREE` | no | `/agents` | Container path of the bind-mounted tree. Per agent: `<tree>/<id>/workspace` and `<tree>/<id>/home`. |
# | `AGENT_STATE_ROOT` | no | `<AGENT_TREE>/.state` | Per-agent supervision state (stop markers, logs). Inside the tree, so a stop survives a container restart. |
# | `HERDR_SESSION` | no | `agents` | herdr's named session. Exported, so every child — the server and every pane — resolves the same one. |
# | `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` | no | `$HOME/.config` | The herdr config root. Exported, and written into the SSH daemon's `SetEnv` in the same run, so the two can never disagree. |
# | `AGENT_ENTRYPOINT` | no | `/usr/local/bin/agent-entrypoint` | The base image's entrypoint — what each pane runs for its agent. |
# | `AGENT_PLUGIN_ROOT` | no | `/usr/local/share/agent-host/plugin` | Where the manifest and the two scripts live. |
# | `AGENT_PLUGIN_ID`, `AGENT_PANE_ENTRYPOINT` | no | `agent-respawn`, `agent` | The plugin and pane ids used when opening panes. |
# | `AGENT_HERDR_CONFIG_TEMPLATE` | no | `/usr/local/share/agent-host/herdr-config.toml` | Installed to the config root when it has no `config.toml`. |
# | `AGENT_SSH_ENABLE` | no | `1` | Whether to bring up the SSH daemon. |
# | `AGENT_SSH_PORT`, `AGENT_SSH_LISTEN` | no | `2222`, `127.0.0.1` | Port and bind address. The loopback default is the isolation posture: reachable from outside only when the deployment publishes it. |
# | `AGENT_SSHD_DIR`, `AGENT_SSH_AUTHORIZED_KEYS` | no | `$HOME/.sshd`, `<dir>/authorized_keys` | Host key, config, pid and authorized keys. Mount this directory to keep the host identity stable across recreates. |
# | `AGENT_BOOT_FOREGROUND` | no | `1` | `1`: exec the herdr client, so the container's process is the session. `0`: reconcile and exit — the mode the acceptance script uses. |
#
# ## Order, and why it is this order
#
# 1. **Contract and tree** — every id validated, every directory created and
#    checked writable, before anything is started. A host that cannot write an
#    agent's workspace must refuse, not start an agent that will fail later. The
#    id charset is the base's (`[A-Za-z0-9._-]`, first character alphanumeric,
#    64 characters): the id becomes a workspace label, a directory name and a log
#    name, so separators, whitespace and control characters are refused here.
# 2. **Configuration** — the packaged `config.toml` is installed only when the
#    root has none, so an operator's own file is never overwritten.
# 3. **SSH daemon** — the `SetEnv` line takes the same `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and
#    `HERDR_SESSION` this process exports, from the same variables, in the same
#    run. Its config is validated with `sshd -t` before it is used; a running
#    daemon is HUP-reloaded rather than duplicated.
# 4. **Server** — `setsid herdr server` starts it as its own session leader,
#    which is the `detached_server_daemon` property `herdr --remote` checks
#    before attaching. If a server for the session already runs, nothing is
#    started. The script then reads the server's own session and logs a warning
#    if it is not its own leader — a host that will accept local clients but
#    refuse remote attach.
# 5. **Plugin** — linked from the packaged root if `plugin list` does not name it.
# 6. **Workspaces** — for each agent id, in the order given: close every existing
#    workspace labelled with that id, create one with the agent's workspace as
#    cwd and the id as its label, append the roster line *before* opening the
#    pane (the wrapper reads the roster the moment it starts), then open the
#    agent pane and close the workspace's shell pane. An agent whose stop marker
#    is present keeps its shell pane and gets no agent pane: attaching shows a
#    shell in the agent's workspace, not a pruned workspace, and the operator has
#    somewhere to remove the marker.
# 7. **Session** — `exec herdr --session <HERDR_SESSION>`: the container's
#    process becomes a client of the session it brought up.
#
# ## Idempotency
#
# Re-running is the normal case — every container start runs it again. The
# server and the daemon are left alone when they are already up; the config
# template is not re-installed over an existing file; the roster is rewritten
# from scratch (it is derived state, and a stale line is a wrong agent); each
# agent's workspace is closed and recreated, which is how the desired set is
# enforced. Nothing accumulates except herdr's own session state and the
# per-agent supervision state.
#
# ## Failure behaviour
#
# Every refusal exits 78 with one line on stderr prefixed `agent-host-boot: `:
# empty `AGENT_IDS`; an id with a character outside the charset or longer than
# 64; an unwritable tree or workspace directory; `herdr`, `jq` or `setsid`
# missing from `PATH`; the entrypoint or plugin root missing; the config
# template missing when no `config.toml` exists; `sshd` missing while SSH is
# enabled, or rejecting the generated configuration; the herdr session not
# coming up within 15 seconds; a workspace that cannot be created or whose id
# cannot be read.
#
# Two conditions are warnings, not refusals, because the host is still usable:
# a herdr server that is not its own session leader (remote attach will demand a
# restart), and an empty `authorized_keys` (key-only auth will reject every
# connection). Both are printed at boot.
#
# ## Validation
#
# ```
# sh -n agent-host-boot.sh
# AGENT_IDS=a,b AGENT_TREE=/tmp/t AGENT_BOOT_FOREGROUND=0 sh agent-host-boot.sh   # exits 0
# herdr workspace list | jq -r '.result.workspaces[].label'                       # a, then b
# ps -o pid=,sid= -C herdr                                                        # server: pid == sid
# ssh <target> 'echo $XDG_CONFIG_HOME $HERDR_SESSION; herdr session list --json'   # parity, session running
# ```
